      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the ground coriander, cumin, turmeric, cinnamon and black pepper together in a small bowl. Rub the spice mixture all over the chicken thighs and set aside for 10 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the olive oil in a dutch oven over medium-high heat. Brown the chicken thighs in batches, skin-side down first, for 4-5 minutes per side until golden. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to medium. Add the sliced onions to the pot and cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring occasionally, until softened and lightly golden."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic and cook for 1 minute more, then stir in the chopped preserved lemon rind."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the chicken to the pot. Pour in enough water to come halfway up the chicken, around 200ml. Bring to a gentle simmer, cover with the lid and cook over low heat for 45 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the green olives and continue cooking, uncovered, for a further 10-15 minutes until the sauce has reduced slightly and the chicken is cooked through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Scatter over the chopped parsley and fresh coriander just before serving. Serve with couscous or warm flatbreads."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The sauce is too salty"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Preserved lemons vary greatly in saltiness. Always rinse the rind thoroughly before use and taste the sauce before adding any extra seasoning. A squeeze of fresh lemon juice at the end can balance the saltiness."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The chicken skin is not browning"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make sure the pot is properly hot before adding the chicken and that the pieces are dry. Pat them with kitchen paper before seasoning. Avoid crowding the pan by browning in batches."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The sauce is too thin"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the lid for the last 15 minutes of cooking to allow the liquid to reduce. If it is still thin, remove the chicken and simmer the sauce alone for a few minutes until it coats the back of a spoon."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chicken tagine with olives and "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "preserved-lemon-term"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "preserved lemon"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is one of Morocco's most recognised dishes, eaten from Marrakech to Fez in homes and market stalls alike. The combination of salty, sharp citrus rind with buttery olives and warm spices is a cornerstone of Moroccan cooking. Traditionally it would be made in a conical clay tagine set over charcoal, though a dutch oven on the hob produces excellent results. The dish is typically served family-style with plenty of bread for mopping up the broth."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
